spi: register the EA3's BCM53125 switch without a device tree

The Control4 EA3 puts a Broadcom BCM53125 managed switch on the CE5310's SPI
bus at chip-select 1. The two rear RJ45s are switch ports; the e1000 MAC is the
CPU port behind it, which is why the stock kernel logs

    GBE working in Internal Fake Phy Mode
    spi-bcm53125 spi0.1: spi_bcm53125_probe: BCM53125 SPI Driver

Mainline already has both halves of the driver stack: spi-pxa2xx-pci binds the
PCI function (8086:2e6a, the CE4100 port, which the CE5310 also exposes) and
b53_spi drives the part. What is missing is the board description — "there is a
bcm53125 at chip-select 1 of bus 0, and here is how its ports map" — which
normally comes from DT, and our CEFDK bootlinux boot passes no DTB.

Same problem, and the same style of fix, as 0001-i2c-pxa-pci: describe the
child device in code instead of in a device tree.

This uses spi_register_board_info(), the mechanism built for exactly this, so
no existing driver has to be modified: the board info sits on the queue until a
controller with bus_num 0 registers, and the SPI core instantiates the device
then. subsys_initcall ordering puts the registration ahead of the PCI driver's
device_initcall.

THIS PATCH ADDS ONE NEW FILE AND NOTHING ELSE — deliberately. An earlier version
also carried a hunk adding `obj-y += spi-ea3-board.o` to drivers/spi/Makefile,
and it failed to apply: the context was written from a guess at that file's
first lines rather than from the real tree, and `Hunk #1 FAILED at 1` killed the
build. A pure file-add needs no context and cannot drift.

The Makefile line is appended instead by LINUX_POST_PATCH_HOOKS in
board/external.mk, which is context-free and self-guarding (it only fires when
this patch has actually put the file there, so EA1 builds are unaffected).

>>> DISABLED — DOES NOT COMPILE AGAINST 7.1.8 <<<

Renamed to `.patch.disabled` so Buildroot skips it. Risk #1 below was not a
hypothetical: the compiler settled it.

    drivers/spi/spi-ea3-board.c:69: variable 'ea3_switch_pdata' has
                                    initializer but incomplete type
    drivers/spi/spi-ea3-board.c:70: 'struct dsa_platform_data' has no
                                    member named 'nr_chips'

THE LEGACY DSA PLATFORM-DATA PATH IS GONE in 7.1.8 — `struct dsa_platform_data`
survives only as a forward declaration, so it cannot be instantiated at all.
Everything else in this patch (the file add, the Makefile hook, the measured
port map) is good; only the *port description mechanism* is dead.

TO REVIVE THIS: describe the ports as a software-node (fwnode) graph and attach
it to the SPI device, replacing dsa_chip_data/dsa_platform_data. The measured
map below carries over unchanged — CPU is port 5, jacks are ports 1 and 2. Then
rename this file back to `.patch` and re-enable the CONFIG_NET_DSA block in
board/ea3/linux/ea3.fragment (the two must move together).

Original risk list, for the record — #1 is now confirmed, #2 and #3 untested:

  1. THE DSA PLATFORM-DATA PATH STILL EXISTS. -> IT DOES NOT. See above.
  2. THE PORT MAP IS MEASURED, but the DSA *bring-up* of an isolated port is
     not. Port 1 is deliberately walled off from the CPU port by the stock
     configuration, so DSA has to reconfigure the switch, not merely describe
     it. That reconfiguration has never been exercised here.
  3. spi_register_board_info() must still exist and still be honoured for
     controllers that register later. It is long-standing but is legacy API.

MEASURED ON A LIVE EA3, via the vendor driver's register sysfs
(/sys/bus/spi/devices/spi0.1/{port,page_reg,value,mibs}):

  * Link Status Summary (page 0x01 reg 0x00) = 0x24 -> ports 2 and 5 up.
  * Per-port MIB counters show ports 2 and 5 as exact mirrors of each other
    (port 5 TxOctets ~= port 2 RxGoodOctets and vice versa) — the signature of
    a CPU-port/external-port pair carrying the same traffic.
  * Port 5's GMII override (page 0x00 reg 0x5d) = 0x4b: link up, full duplex,
    1000M, forced — i.e. the fixed "internal fake phy" link to the SoC MAC.
  * The driver clamps a port selector above 5 to 8, so valid ports are 0-5, 8.
    Port 8 (IMP) reports all-zero counters and no link.

So: CPU PORT IS 5, not the IMP at 8, and the jack this unit's cable was in is
PORT 2. Both are measurements, not guesses.

A controlled test settled it: snapshot every port, push 4 MB out of the box,
snapshot again. Port 5 gained 4,313,853 received bytes, port 2 gained 4,313,811
transmitted bytes, and ports 0/1/3/4 were flat. A 42-byte difference across 4 MB
is framing.

THE SECOND JACK IS PORT 1, found by moving the cable. It is ISOLATED in the
stock configuration: with a cable in it the port links and receives (1.4 MB,
4565 multicast frames) but its TxOctets stays at exactly 0 and the host is
unreachable. Ports 0/1/3/4 all report the same integrated GPHY (0362:5f24), so
silicon presence could not have separated them — only the cable move could.

Also solid: the chip-select and bus number. The stock kernel enumerates the
switch as spi0.1, so bus 0 / CS 1 is read off real hardware. The SPI mode and
clock below are conservative defaults, not values taken from the vendor driver.

Signed-off-by: openHC <openhc@localhost>
---
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-ea3-board.c
@@ -0,0 +1,107 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * Board glue for the Control4 EA3 (Intel Atom CE5310).
+ *
+ * The EA3 has a Broadcom BCM53125 managed switch on SPI bus 0, chip-select 1.
+ * Its two external ports are the unit's rear RJ45s; the on-SoC e1000 MAC hangs
+ * off switch PORT 5 (not the IMP at port 8 — measured, see below) on a fixed
+ * forced link, "internal fake phy" in the vendor's words. Without DSA the
+ * primary jack works and the second one is dark: the stock switch config never
+ * forwards port 1 to the CPU port.
+ *
+ * Bringing the switch up under DSA gives each jack its own netdev, which is the
+ * point: the vendor's out-of-tree spi-bcm53125 driver never did.
+ *
+ * There is no device tree on this machine — CEFDK's bootlinux hands the kernel
+ * no DTB — so the switch is described here in code. spi_register_board_info()
+ * is the mechanism intended for that: the entry waits on a queue until a
+ * controller with the matching bus_num registers, then the SPI core creates the
+ * device. Registering at subsys_initcall puts us ahead of spi-pxa2xx-pci.
+ *
+ * See board/ea3/patches/linux/0003-*.patch in openHC for what is verified here
+ * and what is not. In short: the bus, chip-select and the whole port map were
+ * measured on a live unit; what is untested is DSA's bring-up of them.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/init.h>
+#include <linux/kernel.h>
+#include <linux/spi/spi.h>
+#include <net/dsa.h>
+
+/* Read off a live EA3: the stock kernel enumerates the switch as spi0.1. */
+#define EA3_SWITCH_BUS		0
+#define EA3_SWITCH_CS		1
+
+/* Conservative. The vendor driver's actual clock was not recovered. */
+#define EA3_SWITCH_HZ		1000000
+
+/*
+ * Port map for the BCM53125.
+ *
+ * port_names[] is indexed by switch port number. "cpu" marks the port wired to
+ * the host MAC; every other named port becomes its own netdev.
+ *
+ * PORT 5 IS THE CPU PORT, NOT THE IMP AT 8. Measured on a live EA3 through the
+ * vendor driver's register sysfs: link summary (page 0x01 reg 0x00) reads 0x24
+ * — ports 2 and 5 — and those two ports' MIB counters are exact mirrors of
+ * each other. Port 5's GMII override (page 0x00 reg 0x5d) reads 0x4b: forced
+ * link, 1000M, full duplex, which is the fixed "internal fake phy" link the
+ * vendor kernel announces. Port 8 shows no link and all-zero counters.
+ *
+ * PORT 2 is the primary jack, also measured: pushing 4 MB out of the box moved
+ * 4,313,853 bytes into port 5 and 4,313,811 bytes out of port 2, with every
+ * other port flat.
+ *
+ * PORT 1 is the second jack, measured by moving the cable. Note that it is
+ * ISOLATED in the stock configuration: with a cable in it the port links and
+ * receives (1.4 MB, 4565 multicast frames) but its TxOctets stays at exactly 0
+ * and the host is unreachable. Bringing it up as a real netdev is precisely
+ * what this DSA setup is for — the vendor never exposed it.
+ */
+#define EA3_SWITCH_CPU_PORT	5
+
+static struct dsa_chip_data ea3_switch_chip = {
+	.port_names[1]			= "lan1",	/* isolated in stock cfg */
+	.port_names[2]			= "lan2",	/* the primary jack       */
+	.port_names[EA3_SWITCH_CPU_PORT]	= "cpu",	/* to the SoC e1000       */
+};
+
+static struct dsa_platform_data ea3_switch_pdata = {
+	.nr_chips	= 1,
+	.chip		= &ea3_switch_chip,
+};
+
+static struct spi_board_info ea3_spi_devices[] __initdata = {
+	{
+		.modalias	= "bcm53125",
+		.bus_num	= EA3_SWITCH_BUS,
+		.chip_select	= EA3_SWITCH_CS,
+		.max_speed_hz	= EA3_SWITCH_HZ,
+		.mode		= SPI_MODE_0,
+		.platform_data	= &ea3_switch_pdata,
+	},
+};
+
+static int __init ea3_board_init(void)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = spi_register_board_info(ea3_spi_devices,
+				      ARRAY_SIZE(ea3_spi_devices));
+	if (ret) {
+		pr_warn("ea3-board: could not register bcm53125 at spi%d.%d: %d\n",
+			EA3_SWITCH_BUS, EA3_SWITCH_CS, ret);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	pr_info("ea3-board: bcm53125 queued for spi%d.%d\n",
+		EA3_SWITCH_BUS, EA3_SWITCH_CS);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * subsys_initcall, not device_initcall: the board info has to be on the queue
+ * before spi-pxa2xx-pci registers the controller, or the SPI core will have
+ * nothing to match and the switch is never created.
+ */
+subsys_initcall(ea3_board_init);
